WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.360
MCP needs operational governance, not only developer experimentation.

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:08.120
A server inventory should record which MCP servers exist, who owns them,

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:11.090
where they came from, what version is running, what dependencies they use,

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:12.755
and which environments allow them.

00:00:12.755 --> 00:00:17.440
A tool inventory should record purpose, permissions, data sensitivity, downstream systems,

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:19.415
approval requirements, and current status.

00:00:19.415 --> 00:00:22.490
Approved servers and disabled or unused tools should be visible,

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:25.815
not hidden in individual workstations or team experiments.

00:00:25.815 --> 00:00:29.130
Monitoring should cover tool calls, server connections, resource access,

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:34.115
authorization decisions, user approvals, policy denials, errors, and unusual use.

00:00:34.115 --> 00:00:37.160
These events help teams troubleshoot problems and investigate incidents.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:40.125
For example, if a tool starts failing after a server upgrade,

00:00:40.125 --> 00:00:43.000
operations needs version and error information.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.470
If a sensitive resource is accessed unexpectedly, security needs user, tool, server,

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:49.040
and policy context.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:53.105
Observability turns MCP behavior into something the organization can manage.

00:00:53.105 --> 00:00:54.820
Change management is part of scaling.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:57.990
Server updates, new tools, permission changes, dependency updates,

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:00.640
and transport changes should be reviewed according to risk.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.845
A read-only documentation resource may need a light process.

00:01:03.845 --> 00:01:07.975
A write-capable tool connected to production systems needs stronger review, testing,

00:01:07.975 --> 00:01:10.115
approval, and rollback planning.

00:01:10.115 --> 00:01:13.290
Incident response readiness should include how to disable a tool, block a server,

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:17.840
revoke a token, preserve evidence, and identify affected users or systems.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.040
Governance should be practical rather than ceremonial.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:24.010
Teams can scale MCP adoption by defining ownership, documenting approved patterns,

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:27.730
reviewing dependencies, limiting unused tools, logging important events,

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:30.200
and training users on approval flows.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.725
MCP can make AI integrations more consistent, auditable, and maintainable,

00:01:33.725 --> 00:01:37.090
but only when teams keep visibility over what is connected, who can use it,

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:39.080
and what happens when something goes wrong.
